{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on ceaa2cf1: the interning half is worth landing under review, the checkpoint half is not yet decided, and nothing is discarded. Evidence first, because planner explicitly did not verify any of it and asked me not to take the comments on trust.\n\nPreserved before judged. The 235 insertions are now commit 45f40a89 on request/resident-memory-500k. That branch is already an ancestor of main, so the commit lands nothing and adds no code to any shipped artifact; it exists so the delta stops living only in a working tree that every ref-based view says is finished.\n\nThe claim that would have killed it holds. The comment asserts the interned projection is byte-for-byte identical. I built gs from adda28c8 and from 45f40a89 in separate worktrees, folded this actual log through each, and compared: 17,735,545 bytes of JSON from both, SHA-256 41f0ed663f5add0d6b8a5e5206218b4e76627a8aa9ce7f014f3ed4f483d9ffb6 from both, over 8,056 events. I checked the binaries were genuinely different before believing that, because I have compared a build against itself before and read the result as evidence: they differ, and the intern symbols appear five times in the candidate and zero times in the baseline.\n\nWhat it buys, three runs each at both commits:\n\n  RosterHeavy/5000        149.5MB -\u003e 129.9MB (-13.1%)   761,447 -\u003e 646,586 allocs (-15.1%)   +2.5% time\n  RequestHeavy/20000      107.7MB -\u003e  92.6MB (-14.0%)   640,752 -\u003e 540,748 allocs (-15.6%)   +3.7% time\n  KindChurn/1600           63.7MB -\u003e  59.1MB ( -7.2%)   381,496 -\u003e 357,588 allocs ( -6.3%)   +5.6% time\n  FolderAppendToProjection 38.6MB -\u003e  38.6MB ( -0.0%)        65,070 -\u003e 65,065 ( -0.0%)   -1.1% time\n\nThe last line is the one that shapes the answer and is easy to skip past. Interning does nothing for incremental append, which is the path a running resident spends its life in. The win is entirely in cold fold. So this is a startup-and-rebuild optimization, not a steady-state one, and anyone describing it as reducing resident memory generally would be overselling it. Against the 500k envelope this lane was chasing it is still material: this log folds to 17.7MB at 8k events, so a 500k log is order-of-gigabyte cold, and 13% of that is worth having.\n\nMy recommendation is land the interning under review, and I want the review aimed at one thing in particular rather than at the numbers. The saving comes from a custom JSON decode path that lets encoding/json validate the payload while the fold adopts the unescaped bytes without copying. That is a correctness-critical surface reached by every record's payload. Byte-identical output over one real log is good evidence and is not proof that no input reaches a divergence; the delta's own TestFolderPooledDecodePreservesCanonicalEscapesAndRejectsUnknownFields exists for exactly that worry and should be attacked rather than admired.\n\nThe checkpoint half I am not deciding. appendBorrowedChunk compresses complete chunks straight from reader-owned memory and clones only the tail. I have not measured what it saves and have not tested the ownership argument, and planner is right that borrowing caller-owned bytes stays correct until an assumption changes quietly. Deciding it needs its own measurement; carrying it along because it happens to sit in the same tree is how unreviewed optimizations get merged.\n\nBoth packages build and their tests pass at 45f40a89.\n\nWhat is left before this can be reviewed, and why it is not trivial. The delta predates main 82e45622 and fold.go changed underneath it when the live-membership merge-receipt guard landed this morning. Carrying it forward is a rebase across that fix, and I have reshipped a fixed defect through exactly this kind of carry before, so the guard needs checking against the carried tree rather than assumed intact - the interning touches the same file the guard lives in.\n\nNot asking anyone to act on this yet. The decision the request wanted is recorded; the carry and the review request follow."}